DILG Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOM Center. EDSA corner Quezon Avenue, West Triangle, Quezon City http:/twww.dilg.com.ph 27 SEP DILG OPIN!ON NO. MAYOR RYANH M. DOLOR Municipality of Bauan Province of Batangas MR. FELIPE D. MARAMOT Property owner Rising Tide Construction Supply Sta. Maria, Bauan, Batangas Dear Gentlemen: This has references to the following letters: 62 Letter dated 20 August 2019 from Mr. Maramot, property owner, requesting for this Department's legal opinion on whether or not the DILG MC No. 2019-121 covers what was once a barangay road but which already became a private property because of an exchange by virtue of sanggunian bayan and sanggunian barangay resolutions; and Letter from 08 August 2019 from Mayor Dolor, Municipality of Bauan, Province of Batangas, requesting for legal opinion on whether a mere Resolution passed by the Sanggunian Bayan is enough to allow a private individual to close an existing barangay road and be replaced by a portion of a privately owned lot. The facts stated in the letters are as follows: 1. 2. 3. A Barangay road in Sitio Bubuyan, Locloc, Bauan was sandwiched between two parcels of land owned by Mr. Maramot; Mr. Maramot suggested that his property on the east side be donated to the barangay in exchange of the part of the barangay road adjacent to his property on the west side; Barangay issued a resolution blessing the construction of the barangay road on the property of Mr. Maramot on the west side;

4. Thereafter, Sanggunian Bayan of Bauan, Batangas also issued a resolution allowing Mr. Maramot to construct a barangay road on his lot. 5. This Department issued Memorandum Circular No. 2019-121 relative to the Presidential Directive during the 4th State of the Nation Address of the President to Clear Roads of Illegal Structures and Constructions. Now, the submitted queries are as follows: 3. Whether or not mere sanggunian resolutions are sufficient to validate and effect the exchange of barangay road with a private property; and 4. Whether or not DILG MC 2019-121 covers then barangay road turned into private property by virtue of said sanggunian barangay and sanggunian bayan resolutions. As to the first query, we opine in the negative. Barangay road is a property for public use and is outside the commerce of man. It cannot be subject of a contractual undertaking. Accordingly, the subject barangay road and property owned by Mr. Maramot would have to be covered by a contractual undertaking for said exchange to be effected. In the case of Dacanay vs. Asistio, the Supreme Court stated that: "There is no doubt that the disputed areas from which the private respondents' market stalls are sought to be evicted are public streets, as found by the trial court in Civil Case No. C-12921. A public street is property for public use hence outside the commerce of man (Arts. 420, 424, Civil Code). Being outside the commerce of man, it may not be the subject of lease or other contract. (Villanueva et al. vs. Castaneda and Macalino, 15 SCRA 142, citing the Municipality of Cavite vs. Rojas, 30 SCRA 602; Espiritu vs. Municipal Council of Pozorrubio, 102 Phil. 869; and Muyot vs. De Ia Fuente, 48 0.G. 4860). " Accordingly, any agreement on the same would be considered void. Being void, any subsequent resolution on the same would have no legal basis. Notwithstanding, we deemed it prudent to emphasize that in this case, the Sanggunian Bayan and Sanggunian Barangay caused Mr. Maramot to believe that said exchange of properties and consequential constructions are legal by virtue of issuances of said resolutions. Also, for seven (7) years from the time the consequential constructions were accomplished, said local governments did not object nor disapprove the same through the non-issuance of building permit, if any. It is also noted that the barangay started using the west-wing property of Mr. Maramot as the new barangay road. Consequently, damages may be sustained on the part of Mr. Maramot if clearing of said structures will be conducted. On the second query, we lean toward the affirmative. The subject property is covered under the DILG MC No. 2019-121. However, please note that what the MC directs the pertinent LGUs to revoke are permits. What was issued here was

not merely a permit but sanggunian resolutions. MC provides that when it comes to legislative measures, such as in this case i.e. Sanggunian resolutions, the local sanggunians are enjoined to revisit to ensure responsiveness to this presidential directive and consistency with related laws and policies. Thus, the respective LGUs herein involved are expected to revisit legislative measures issued for purposes as above-mentioned. Thank you. MARIV Unde LLLS/42 Very truly yours, C. SACENDONCILLO, CESO 111 ecretary for Local Government
